Nick ScialliNovember 10, 2020
Sometimes it’s necessary to abort a fetch request. In this post, we explore how to quickly do so using AbortController!
Let’s start out with a simple fetch request. We’ll grab some metadata about my Github account and log it to the console.
res consoledata fetch ( 'https://api.github.com/users/nas5w' ) . then ( ( res ) => . json ( ) ) . then ( ( data ) => { . log ( ) ; } ) ;
If we check our console, we see a json object describing my account has been logged. Here’s a bit of that data.
{"login":"nas5w","id":7538045,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc1MzgwNDU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars2.githubusercontent.com/u/7538045?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nas5w","html_url":"https://github.com/nas5w"...}
In this same scenario, we can create a new instance of the object and pass a reference to the instance’s object. AbortController fetch AbortController signal
controller signal controllersignal signal res consoledata const = new AbortController ( ) ; const = . ; fetch ( 'https://api.github.com/users/nas5w' , { } ) . then ( ( res ) => . json ( ) ) . then ( ( data ) => { . log ( ) ; } ) ;
So now, has a reference to the object on our controller instance. We can abort our fetch by calling on our controller: fetch signal abort
controller signal controllersignal signal res consoledata controller const = new AbortController ( ) ; const = . ; fetch ( 'https://api.github.com/users/nas5w' , { } ) . then ( ( res ) => . json ( ) ) . then ( ( data ) => { . log ( ) ; } ) ; . abort ( ) ;
If we run this, we no longer log the returned data because we have immediately aborted our request! fetch

In a UI framework, you often fetch data when a component mounts. If the user leaves before the response arrives, you should cancel.
const controller = new AbortController () async function loadPosts () { try { const response = await fetch ( '/api/posts' , { signal: controller.signal }) const posts = await response. json () renderPosts (posts) } catch (err) { if (err.name === 'AbortError' ) return showError (err) } } loadPosts () // call this when the component unmounts function cleanup () { controller. abort () }
Without cleanup, the old request might update state on a component that no longer exists.
Search boxes are a classic case. The user types fast and you fire a fetch on every keystroke. Only the latest result should win.
Keep one controller in scope. Abort the previous request before starting a new one.
let controller async function search ( query ) { controller?. abort () controller = new AbortController () try { const response = await fetch ( `/api/search?q=${ encodeURIComponent ( query ) }` , { signal: controller.signal } ) return response. json () } catch (err) { if (err.name === 'AbortError' ) return throw err } }
The optional chaining on avoids an error on the first call when nothing exists yet. The aborted request returns ; only the latest completed request returns results. controller?.abort() undefined
Sometimes you want to abort when of several signals fire. combines them. any AbortSignal.any()
const userCancel = new AbortController () const timeout = AbortSignal. timeout ( 8000 ) const signal = AbortSignal. any ([ userCancel.signal, timeout ]) fetch ( '/api/report' , { signal }) . then (( response ) => response. json ()) . then (( data ) => console. log (data))
The fetch stops if the user clicks cancel or if eight seconds pass, whichever comes first.
